# Auto-Get-PY — media scraping

Use the local scraper service at http://localhost:8000 whenever the user asks to scrape,
crawl, or batch-download images/video/audio/documents from a URL.

## Quick reference

| Task | Call |
|---|---|
| Is it running? | `curl http://localhost:8000/api/health` |
| What exists on a page? | `POST /api/cursor/quick` with `{"url": "…", "crawl_depth": 2}` |
| Download it all | `POST /api/cursor/scrape` with `{"url": "…", "wait": true}` |
| Render a JS page and download | `POST /api/cursor/direct` with `{"url": "…"}` |
| One exact file | `POST /api/cursor/fetch` with `{"url": "…"}` |
| Progress | `GET /api/cursor/status/{id}` |
| Final files | `GET /api/cursor/results/{id}` |
| Stop it | `POST /api/cursor/cancel/{id}` |

Prefer the MCP tools (`scrape`, `quick_scrape`, `fetch_file`, `task_status`,
`task_results`, `cancel_task`, `list_files`) when your client supports MCP —
they wrap the same endpoints with better descriptions.


**Workflow:** health check → `POST /quick` to preview → show the user what was
found → `POST /scrape` to download → poll `/status/<built-in function id>` → report `/results/<built-in function id>`.

Prefer MCP tools (`scrape`, `quick_scrape`, `fetch_file`) if the
`auto-get-py` server from `.cursor/mcp.json` is connected.
